nodecode-lfg-alarm is a rewrite of lfg-gm's lfg-gm/lfg-alarm-mcp:

  https://github.com/lfg-gm/lfg-alarm-mcp
  commit ba7b6afb27ef9a737986a3e5a57b4b2b66482d91
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 LFG Alarm (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's whole reach -- its mcp.json, which names the hosted
MCP server at https://mcp.lfg.gm over the streamable HTTP transport with
Authorization: Bearer -- and its skill, skills/lfg-alarm/SKILL.md, whose
guidance is the cell's manual topic (help :lfg-alarm). The connection is
JSON-RPC over HTTP from the image (nlk:http): initialize, notifications/
initialized, tools/list (following the server's cursors) and tools/call, a
JSON body or a server-sent event stream in answer, the Mcp-Session-Id and
MCP-Protocol-Version headers the transport asks for, one fresh handshake
when a session the server forgot answers 400 or 404, and a tool's isError
answer reported as the call's failure. Every tool the server lists is
registered as a Nodecode tool under the server's own name with the server's
own description and input schema, so the model sees what Hermes showed it;
the section declares the key, the URL, one request's timeout and how much of
an answer a result carries. The Python-free original is Lisp here, and no
Node, Python or MCP server is launched locally.

Left out, and why: the plugin was only a pointer plus a skill, so the one
thing not reproduced is the OAuth 2.1 sign-in its README walks a person
through for Hermes (dynamic client registration, a browser on lfg.gm and a
callback route). A personal key from the LFG Alarm app reaches the same
server, the same account and the same tools, so the cell takes the key
instead of keeping an OAuth client in a tool cell. Also left out is Hermes's
`trust: untrusted` setting, which is Hermes's own gate on write-capable tool
calls rather than a property of the server. The tools and the data are LFG
Alarm's; the license above covers the plugin, not the service.
